Cactus Code Thorn TargetTracker
Thorn Author(s)     : Jordan Nicoules
                      Miguel Zilhão
Thorn Maintainer(s) : Jordan Nicoules
                      Miguel Zilhão
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

Purpose of the thorn:

Track the locations of a number of targets during an evolution and update the
locations of associated `SphericalSurface`s.

It can be used together with any thorn computing suitable grid scalars, and the
grid scalars computed by TargetTracker itself (`target_loc_*`, `target_com_*`)
can be fed back into it or into other thorns.

The targets are configured with per-target parameters (10 targets by
default, see `nmax_targets`), in the style of `SphericalSurface`.


Tracking targets
----------------

### Basic usage

For each target i (0 <= i < nmax_targets), set `track[i] = yes` and
provide the grid scalars to track with `target_x/y/z[i]`, e.g.

    TargetTracker::track               [0] = yes
    TargetTracker::target_x            [0] = "PunctureTracker::pt_loc_x[0]"
    TargetTracker::target_y            [0] = "PunctureTracker::pt_loc_y[0]"
    TargetTracker::target_z            [0] = "PunctureTracker::pt_loc_z[0]"
    TargetTracker::which_surface_to_store_info[0] = 0

The tracked coordinates are stored in the public grid scalars
`TargetTracker::target_loc_x/y/z[i]` at every tracking iteration
(see `track_every`, `start_tracking_after_time`, `stop_tracking_after_time`).
If `which_surface_to_store_info[i]` is set, the centroid of the given
spherical surface is set to the tracked location, and the surface is
marked as active/valid. This, in particular, moves `SphericalSurface`s
(and thus e.g. `AHFinderDirect`'s or `QuasiLocalMeasures`'s surfaces)
during the evolution, as long as the corresponding grid scalars are
updated. (The user must ensure the surface resolutions `ntheta`/`nphi`
etc. are set.)

A target with `target_*` set to "" is "fixed": it does not move
(its location is set by `initial_x/y/z[i]`).

### Surface-following mode ('surface to tracker')

Setting `loc_from_surface_mode[i] = yes` inverts the data flow: the
tracker does not read grid scalars but instead copies the centroid of
its associated surface into `target_loc`. This is useful to carry a
tracker across the formation of a new horizon: once `AHFinderDirect`
finds the horizon and stores it into the surface, the tracker keeps
the location, continuously, from the surface.

### Tracking the opposite, and ad hoc adjustments

Two methods allow to derive the tracked location from the tracked
scalar(s), for cases not covered by a suitable grid scalar (e.g. when
tracking the mirror of a puncture in a symmetry run):

  - `track_opposite[i] = yes`: track the opposite of the target
    (loc = -target). This takes precedence over the next method.

  - `tracker_adjust[i] = yes` with `tracker_x/y/z_adjust_factor[i]`
    and `tracker_x/y/z_adjust_origin[i]`: apply an affine adjustment
    loc = factor * target + origin, for each direction independently.
    A fixed dimension (empty target name) is not adjusted.

All these parameters are STEERABLE=ALWAYS, and can be steered at
runtime (e.g. with the `Trigger` thorn). The interface quantities
(`target_loc`, `adj_fac`, `adj_ori`) and the relevant internal flags
are recovered from checkpoints; most other parameters are STEERABLE=RECOVER
and taken from the parameter file at recovery. In case of ambiguity at
recovery (a parameter changed during the run and reused identically, or
a user-modified parameter file), the internal values are recovered and
the parameters reset to them, with warnings; `force_params_at_recovery[i] = yes`
reverses this for target i (see the caveats in param.ccl).


Self-contained center of mass
-----------------------------

For each target with `compute_com[i] = yes`, TargetTracker computes the
center of mass of the matter distribution,

    CoM^i = \int x^i rho alpha sqrt(gamma) dV / \int rho alpha sqrt(gamma) dV,

with rho = - n^mu n^nu T_mu_nu built from the stress-energy tensor of
`TmunuBase`. It is scheduled to run *before* the tracking itself, so that the
results can be tracked in the same pass. The results are stored in the public,
checkpointed grid scalars

    TargetTracker::target_com_x/y/z[i]   (center of mass)
    TargetTracker::target_com_mass[i]    (mass integral, i.e. the denominator)

and can be output, tracked (with the parameters `target_*[i]`, by this
thorn or any other), or used to move the corresponding puncture/surface.

Restricting the integral to a spherical shell centered at the tracked
point is done with `com_inside_radius[i]` and `com_outside_radius[i]`
(negative = no restriction):

    com_outside_radius[i] < r <= com_inside_radius[i]

with r the coordinate distance to `target_loc_*[i]`. The (inclusive
inside, exclusive outside) conventions are complementary, so that
inside + outside integrals add up to the global one. This is useful
in particular for tracking the matter around a given object (e.g.
the collapsing scalar cluster of a hairy BH evolution), and the sphere
moves with the tracker.

The integrands are computed on all refinement levels at once (a single
set of grid functions is recomputed and reduced sequentially for each
target, in the spirit of `VolumeIntegrals_GRMHD`), and reduced with the
driver's sum reduction, which handles mesh refinement. The following
global parameters control the calculation:

  - `com_every`: do the calculation every N iterations (for all
    targets together; 0 disables). Note that the results are stored in
    the grid scalars between calculations (checkpointed), and that the
    calculation requires the stress-energy tensor of the current pass
    (the user must make sure `TmunuBase` storage is on and the tensor
    is up to date, e.g. with `TmunuBase::stress_energy_at_RHS = yes`).

  - `excise_horizon`: exclude the region inside the (apparent) horizon
    from the integration. This relies on the mask set by `AHFinderDirect`
    (SpaceMask type "mask", state "outside"), similarly to `UAv_Analysis`.

  - `com_symmetry`: multiply the integrands by the appropriate factors
    for bitant runs ("none", "bitant_x/y/z"), as in `UAv_Analysis`.

If the mass integral vanishes (e.g. an empty sphere), the previous
values are kept and a warning printed (the calculation becomes trivially
ill-defined).

Since the integrands are private to the thorn, the grid functions do not
need to be sync'ed between calculations for correctness of the reduction;
they are nevertheless synced to make their (ghost) values meaningful for
output. The reduction follows the driver's weights (in particular,
half-weighting grid layers on symmetry planes, as required to normalize
symmetric integrals).


Schedule placement
------------------

The thorn runs at ANALYSIS (as does e.g. `Trigger_Check`, which runs before it).
The CoM calculation thus runs before the tracking, so that the grid scalars it
produces can be tracked in the same pass (by this thorn or others). Note the
one-pass lag for a *self-tracking* restriction window (the sphere is centered on
the previous pass's tracked point): same feedback design as
`VolumeIntegrals_GRMHD`'s moving spheres. The `after
AHFinderDirect_maybe_do_masks` (used by `UAv_Analysis`) is not part of this
schedule: with `excise_horizon = yes`, the user must make sure the mask is up to
date at ANALYSIS (e.g. run `AHFinderDirect` at CCTK_POSTSTEP, as in its default
configuration, or at ANALYSIS before this thorn — see the discussion in the
schedule.ccl of both thorns).

Other schedule locations: `ParamCheck` (parameter checks), `INITIAL`
and `CCTK_POST_RECOVER_VARIABLES` (initialization, recovery),
`CCTK_INITIAL`/`CCTK_POST_RECOVER_VARIABLES` (counter safety),
`BASEGRID` (flags for the CoM: multipatch detection, symmetry factors).


Known limitations
-----------------

  - 10 targets maximum (crutch parameter `nmax_targets`).

  - The thorn is scheduled at ANALYSIS only (no POSTSTEP option as of
    yet; see the TODO in schedule.ccl). As with `UAv_Analysis`, care
    must be taken with schedule bins and MoL substeps if derivatives
    or reductions are involved.

  - The CoM restriction is a sphere centered at the tracked point; the
    radius is a fixed parameter (in particular it does not follow a
    possibly non-spherical surface). Arbitrary integrands, and other
    integral quantities (energy, angular momentum, quadrupole...) are
    not provided: see `UAv_Analysis` and `VolumeIntegrals_GRMHD`.

  - Only Carpet(-style) drivers are supported/relevant (in particular
    for the reduction weights), and no accelerator offloading is done.

  - Checkpoint recovery of parameters: STEERABLE=ALWAYS parameters are
    recovered through checkpointed internal flags (see the recovery
    section above and the caveats therein). STEERABLE=RECOVER parameters
    are taken from the parameter file. The tracker grid scalars
    (`target_loc`, `target_com`) are checkpointed as well.

  - The testsuite deliberately does not cover checkpoint recovery: at the
    time of writing, restarting any evolution run with this configuration
    (even a plain `ScalarEvolve` run, with this thorn disabled) aborts in
    `CarpetIOHDF5::Recover` (a `CarpetLib` timelevel assertion), i.e. a
    pre-existing driver issue rather than a TargetTracker one.
